{
  "description": "JSON bodies of the 1.3 messages (Pad's input protocol v2). Each case decodes 'json' as 'type' with the production decoder and encodes it again; the result must equal 'canonical' (or 'json' when absent) as a JSON value: key order is ignored, numbers compare numerically (15 = 15.0). Canonical form: absent optional members stay absent; members with a default are written (replaceBefore, modifiers, phase, labels, params, dropped, schema, revision); keyShortcut 'repeat' is written only when true; layoutInfo 'name' defaults to 'id'; a negative replaceBefore reads as 0; notice params that are not strings become their JSON text. Unknown members are ignored. Capability-gated messages decode strictly: every 'invalid' body must fail (unknown enum values, modifier bits above 15, unknown named keys, phases that do not fit the kind, missing companions, positions outside 0...1). 'serverNotices' is the ServerState.notices array, 'notice' one entry, 'source' ServerState.source. Cases named 'tolerant: …' pin host-to-client tolerance: a notices entry that is not an object with a string code is skipped, notices that are not a list (object, null) read as no notices, notice params that are not an object read as {}, and a profilesSnapshot entry that is not a valid profile (a string, a number, null) is skipped. A JSON array is never read as an object (no positional decoding), at the top level or nested: where a message has an object, an array is an error ('invalid'), except where tolerance skips the entry (an array in notices or in a snapshot's profiles is skipped). Tolerance stops there: a ServerState.source that is not an object and a snapshot whose 'profiles' is not a list are errors (see 'invalid'); a client treats such a source as absent. 'json' is the body as a JSON value, except in the 'duplicate key: …' cases: there it is the body's text as a JSON string, because a parsed fixture file cannot keep a repeated key. Pass such a string to wire.decode unchanged (the method table reads a string body as its text) or as the text to a typed decoder. A key repeated in one object keeps its last value, at any depth. 'source' on a case says which set it came from.",
